From: Salty <hoffman.viking@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 02:30:51 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] tv: run the next-episode lookups in parallel too

The probe went parallel but the next-episode half (one or two grouped
statements plus a full-row materialisation per series) still ran one
series at a time and dominated what was left. Ordered PLINQ keeps the
output order and the null filtering identical; the trade is bounded
over-computation past the page cut, which a two-dozen-series set never
reaches.
---
 Emby.Server.Implementations/TV/TVSeriesManager.cs | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Emby.Server.Implementations/TV/TVSeriesManager.cs b/Emby.Server.Implementations/TV/TVSeriesManager.cs
index a992a018..b99e18cc 100644
--- a/Emby.Server.Implementations/TV/TVSeriesManager.cs
+++ b/Emby.Server.Implementations/TV/TVSeriesManager.cs
@@ -139,7 +139,17 @@ namespace Emby.Server.Implementations.TV
                     .OrderByDescending(i => i.LastWatchedDate);
             }
 
+            // The next-episode lookup is the heavier half (one or two grouped
+            // statements plus a full-row materialisation per series) and was still
+            // sequential after the probe went parallel. Same treatment: ordered
+            // PLINQ keeps the output order and the null filtering identical.
+            // shortcut: this computes every candidate's episode even when the page
+            // cut would have skipped some; revisit with a bounded prefix if a
+            // library ever has hundreds of in-progress series.
             return allNextUp
+                .AsParallel()
+                .AsOrdered()
+                .WithDegreeOfParallelism(degree)
                 .Select(i => i.GetEpisodeFunction())
                 .Where(i => i is not null)!;
         }
